      ],
      "quote": "(2) Covered nation.-The term \"covered nation\" means- (A) the Democratic People's Republic of North Korea; (B) the People's Republic of China; (C) the Russian Federation; and (D) the Islamic Republic of Iran.",
      "source": "usc_4872"
    },
    "materials": [
      {
        "material": "Gallium",
        "use": "Integrated circuits took 73% of US gallium consumption, and USGS says their uses “included defense applications, high-performance computers, and telecommunications equipment”. A Department of War release says of gallium and scandium together that “missile defense platforms, sensors, fighter aircraft, and hypersonic weapons all require these elements”.",
        "use_sources": [
          "mcs_gallium",
          "dow_gallium"
        ],
        "law": "Added to the materials covered by 10 U.S.C. 4872, with effect from 18 December 2027. On the NATO list.",
        "law_sources": [
          "pl_119_60",
          "nato"
        ],
        "supplier": "No public source found names a supplier of gallium to a defence programme.",
        "supplier_sources": [],
        "grades": {
          "pathway": "B",
          "supplier": "D",
          "quantity": "D"
        },
        "substitutes": "USGS: in many defence-related applications “no effective substitutes exist for GaAs and GaN”.",
        "stockpile": "Not in the fiscal 2025 purchase plan.",
        "capacity": "USGS lists primary production capacity of 120 tonnes a year outside China, 10 of it in Russia, against output of 9 tonnes. Rio Tinto says that if a trial succeeds it plans a demonstration plant in Quebec of up to 3.5 tonnes a year; it gives no start date (company statement).",
        "replace_sources": [
          "mcs_gallium",
          "dla_plan",
          "rio_tinto"
        ]
      },
      {
        "material": "Germanium",
        "use": "USGS lists US end uses, in descending order, as fibre optics, infrared optics, semiconductor applications and solar cells, and radiation detectors. A Department of War release names “infrared optics, night vision systems, surveillance windows, individual thermal weapon sights” and solar cells for military and civilian satellites.",
        "use_sources": [
          "mcs_germanium",
          "dow_germanium"
        ],
        "law": "Added to the materials covered by 10 U.S.C. 4872, with effect from 18 December 2027. On the NATO list.",
        "law_sources": [
          "pl_119_60",
          "nato"
        ],
        "supplier": "No public source found names a supplier of germanium to a defence programme. For the US market as a whole, USGS estimates that imports of germanium metal fell by 67% in 2025 “owing to China's ban on germanium exports to the United States in 2024”.",
        "supplier_sources": [
          "mcs_germanium"
        ],
        "grades": {
          "pathway": "B",
          "supplier": "D",
          "quantity": "D"
        },
        "substitutes": "USGS: chalcogenide glass has been used in place of germanium metal in infrared applications, and silicon in some electronics.",
        "stockpile": "The fiscal 2025 plan listed 5,000 kilograms for possible sale and none for purchase. A recycling programme was expected to cover roughly 10% of US demand for purified germanium in 2023 (GAO).",
        "capacity": "A US award is to expand one refiner's capacity for germanium metal “sevenfold to more than 20 metric tons annually”; no date is given.",
        "replace_sources": [
          "mcs_germanium",
          "dla_disposals",
          "gao_stockpile",
          "dow_germanium"
        ]
      },
      {
        "material": "Antimony",
        "use": "USGS gives US uses as metal products, including flame retardants, 49%, “antimonial lead and ammunition, 40%”, and nonmetal products 11%. A Department of Defense release says antimony trisulfide is used for “small arms and medium caliber cartridges, as well as many other missile and munition items”. A Department of War release says antimony metal and antimony trisulfide are used in primer production and case hardening.",
        "use_sources": [
          "mcs_antimony",
          "dod_antimony_2022",
          "dow_antimony_2025"
        ],
        "law": "Not named in 10 U.S.C. 4872, in Public Law 119-60 or in the procurement regulation. Not on the NATO list. The table of shortfall materials in the 2021 White House review lists four application areas for antimony, none of them munitions. Antimony is in the stockpile purchase plan.",
        "law_sources": [
          "usc_4872",
          "nato",
          "white_house_2021"
        ],
        "supplier": "GAO reports that in fiscal 2020 the Defense Logistics Agency funded the qualification of a US company sourcing antimony trisulfide from Mexico “to replace the only qualified source located in China”.",
        "supplier_sources": [
          "gao_stockpile"
        ],
        "grades": {
          "pathway": "B",
          "supplier": "A",
          "quantity": "D"
        },
        "supplier_scope": "sole qualified source of one product; purchases not documented",
        "substitutes": "USGS names substitutes for flame retardants, pigments and battery alloys. It does not mention ammunition. Recycling supplied 12% of US apparent consumption.",
        "stockpile": "The fiscal 2025 plan allowed purchases of up to 700 tonnes.",
        "capacity": "One US project reports work to keep it on track to start gold and antimony production in 2029 (company statement). USGS reports that one company began mining antimony in Montana in 2025 and that one US smelter made an estimated 700 tonnes from imported feedstock.",
        "replace_sources": [
