      {
        "material": "Tungsten",
        "use": "USGS estimates that 60% of US tungsten use is in cemented carbide parts for cutting and wear. A Department of Defense release calls tungsten “a critical input for military-grade steel production, aerospace components, munitions, and ground vehicle armor”. The European Commission's research centre writes that it is required for armour-piercing ammunition.",
        "use_sources": [
          "mcs_tungsten",
          "dod_tungsten",
          "jrc"
        ],
        "law": "Tungsten metal powder and tungsten heavy alloy are covered by 10 U.S.C. 4872 now. From 1 January 2027 the statute also bars contracts for covered material mined, refined or separated in a named nation. The procurement regulation, last amended in 2024, has not yet been revised to that text. On the NATO list.",
