      {
        "material": "Rare-earth magnets",
        "use": "USGS says the leading global use of rare earths is magnets. A Department of Defense release calls the magnets “essential components in the F-35, Unmanned Aerial Vehicles, and numerous other defense systems” and adds that these uses “represent a small part of the US commercial need”.",
        "use_sources": [
          "mcs_rare_earths",
          "dod_magnets"
        ],
        "law": "Samarium-cobalt and neodymium-iron-boron magnets are covered by 10 U.S.C. 4872 now. From 1 January 2027 the statute also bars contracts for covered material mined, refined or separated in a named nation. The procurement regulation, last amended in 2024, has not yet been revised to that text. Rare earth elements are on the NATO list.",
        "law_sources": [
          "usc_4872",
          "pl_119_60",
          "dfars",
          "nato"
        ],
        "supplier": "GAO reports a defence official's statement that the F-35 programme “experienced 6-month production stoppages, in 2023 and 2024, when Chinese magnets were identified on the aircraft and had to be replaced”. GAO describes a pause in manufacturing of several months to identify alternative suppliers, and two national security waivers under which aircraft with the magnets were accepted.",
